# Coil — The assets this build produces

Coil ships with no art and no sound, and this build makes both. The snake is
drawn from a produced sprite set and the game plays produced audio, and every one
of those files is generated during this build, committed to the repository, and
bundled by it. Everything else the game shows stays drawn in code, and the last
section of this file lists it.

Producing the assets is a one-time step. `npm run build` bundles the committed
files and nothing more: it does not run the generation binaries, which are on the
`PATH` while this build is being made and absent when the project is built again
from its committed source. A build that shells out to a generation binary fails
wherever those binaries are absent, even though the game is complete.

## The binaries

Six asset-generation binaries are on the `PATH`, and Coil uses four of them.

| Binary | Produces | Used for |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `draw` | one sprite, as a PNG | The straight body, corner, and tail cells |
| `draw-sheet` | a sprite sheet, one PNG per frame | The snake's head and its bite |
| `sfx-synth` | a synthesized sound, as a `.wav` | The eat, combo, and death cues |
| `sfx-sample` | a sound layered over a baked sample pack, as a `.wav` | The same three cues, where a sampled layer suits one better |
| `music` | sequenced music over a baked instrument bank, as a `.wav` and a `.mid` | The music bed |
| `particle-2d` | a particle system, as a `system.json` | Nothing Coil requires. It is available for a flourish of your own. |

Each is a command-line tool, and its own `--help` is its contract; run
`<binary> --help` for its operations and `<binary> <operation> --help` for one
operation's flags. Each records the operations it is given and then renders the
finished file, which you write into the repository at the path this file names.

Produce these files with these binaries. Pre-made art, a downloaded sound, or a
bundled font standing in for a produced file does not meet this contract.

## Where the files land, and how they are loaded

Every produced file sits under `assets/` at the root of this repository, at the
path named below, and is committed.

{{#if (eq engine.slug "none")}}
The build loads them itself. Every URL it requests resolves against the page
rather than against the origin root, so the site runs unchanged whether it is
served from the root of a static host or mounted under a sub-path. A
root-absolute URL such as `/assets/snake/body.png` does not meet this: it
resolves against the origin and fails under a sub-path.
{{else}}
The {{engine.name}} engine provides the asset loader and the audio cue bus, both
documented in the `engine/` directory at the root of this repository. The loader
resolves every path under one root, `assets/`, relative to the page the build is
served from, so the build asks it for a path written relative to that root, such
as `snake/body.png` and `audio/eat.wav`, rather than constructing a URL of its
own. Each audio cue in `specs/ui.md` is bound to its produced file through the
cue bus.
{{/if}}

A load that fails leaves the game running. The game still initializes, still
ticks, still takes input, and still draws a legible board when a sprite or a
sound is unavailable, so a missing file costs the game its polish rather than its
playability.

## The snake sprite set

The snake is drawn from produced sprites rather than from shapes drawn in code.
Each is pixel art on a transparent, straight-alpha canvas of `CELL x CELL`
(`32 x 32`), so one sprite covers exactly one board cell. They are drawn at that
native size and sampled without smoothing, so the pixel art stays sharp at every
scale the stage is fitted to.

| Sprite | Files | Binary | Authored facing |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Head | `assets/snake/head/0.png` to `3.png` | `draw-sheet` | Facing right |
| Straight body | `assets/snake/body.png` | `draw` | A horizontal run |
| Corner body | `assets/snake/corner.png` | `draw` | The bend whose two neighbors lie to the right and below |
| Tail | `assets/snake/tail.png` | `draw` | Its one neighbor lying to the right |

Each sprite is authored in the one orientation the table names and rotated in
quarter turns when it is drawn, so no sprite is produced twice for a second
direction.

### The head sheet

`HEAD_FRAMES` (`4`) frames, one PNG per frame, produced with `draw-sheet` and
emitted as separate files rather than as regions of one image.

| Frame | Pose |
| --- | --- |
| `0` | At rest, mouth closed |
| `1` | Mouth opening |
| `2` | Mouth wide |
| `3` | Mouth closing |

Frame `0` is what the head shows at all times except during a bite. The tick that
eats a pellet starts a bite, which plays frames `1`, `2`, and `3` in order over
`BITE_SECONDS` (`0.25`) and then returns the head to frame `0`. The bite is drawn
only; it changes no rule of the simulation and no timing in `specs/movement.md`.
It runs on the round's own time, so it holds the frame it is on while the game is
paused and carries on from there when the round resumes.

### How a cell picks its sprite

| Board cell | Drawn with |
| --- | --- |
| The head | The head sheet's current frame, turned to the snake's direction |
| A body cell whose two neighbors lie opposite each other | The straight sprite, turned to that run's axis |
| A body cell whose two neighbors lie perpendicular to each other | The corner sprite, turned to that bend |
| The last cell of the chain | The tail sprite, turned toward its one neighbor |

A body cell is never drawn as a bare square, and a turning snake reads as one
continuous coil rather than as a staircase.

### The art bar

- The head is the strongest silhouette on the board. It reads as a head at a
  glance, at one cell, and its facing is unmistakable without reference to the
  body behind it.
- The bite is legible at eight ticks a second. Frame `2` is a visibly open mouth
  rather than a shifted pixel, and the three frames read as one motion.
- The head is clearly brighter or otherwise stronger than the body, so the
  leading cell separates from the trail at any length.
- The straight, corner, and tail sprites join without a seam or a gap, so a
  continuous snake looks continuous where two cells meet.
- Every sprite reads on a dark field, and none of them relies on a background
  behind it.

## The audio

Coil plays four produced sounds, and `specs/ui.md` fixes the events they play on.

| Cue | File | Binary |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `eat` | `assets/audio/eat.wav` | `sfx-synth` or `sfx-sample` |
| `combo-up` | `assets/audio/combo-up.wav` | `sfx-synth` or `sfx-sample` |
| `death` | `assets/audio/death.wav` | `sfx-synth` or `sfx-sample` |
| `music` | `assets/audio/music.wav` | `music` |

`sfx-synth` builds a sound from synth voices alone, and `sfx-sample` layers over
a baked sample pack, which its own operations browse. Use whichever suits each
cue. `music` writes a `.wav` and a `.mid` beside it; the `.wav` is what the game
plays, and keeping the `.mid` is optional.

### The sound bar

- `eat` is short and dry, well under a tick, so eight of them in a second stay
  distinct rather than smearing into one tone.
- `combo-up` is brighter and higher than `eat`, so a player hears the multiplier
  rise on a tick that also plays `eat`.
- `death` is the one heavy sound in the game, clearly longer and lower than the
  other two, and unmistakable as the end of a round.
- `music` is a loop that sits under the game rather than over it. It loops
  without an audible seam and carries no part that competes with the three cues.
- The four together sound like one game, sharing a palette of timbres.

## What stays drawn in code

No produced file covers the following. Each is rendered by the build, and the
file named beside it fixes its behavior and what a player must read.

| Drawn in code | Fixed by |
| --- | --- |
| The board field, its per-cell ruling, and the wall border | `specs/board.md` |
| The obstacle cells, where the mode places them | `specs/mode.md` |
| The pellet | `specs/board.md` |
| The HUD, its readouts, the combo bar, and the mute indicator | `specs/ui.md` |
| Every screen, menu, and panel, and all text | `specs/ui.md` |
| The debug overlay | `specs/instrumentation.md` |
